        #   session 2 (09:38 -> 14:44, 5.11h): ACU, XAI, AIO, BTW
        #                                       = 4 entries = 0.78/hour
        #   combined: 11 entries in 11.25 hours = 0.98/hour ~= 23 PER DAY.
        # So the live bot trades about ONE POSITION PER HOUR, roughly eighty
        # times the rate the handoff assumed, and over those 11 trades it went
        # from $250.00 to $249.41: about -$0.05 PER TRADE.
        # THEREFORE FREQUENCY IS NOT THE CONSTRAINT -- PAYOFF IS. Adding 60%%
        # more candidates to a book that is slightly negative per trade
        # multiplies the loss, and C389 already measured that exact failure on
        # this bot's own data (trades 6->11, WR 83%%->55%%, EV +$0.098->-$0.007).
        # The widening analysis stands and the cost is genuinely small (+17s of
        # a 480s cycle, measured), but it must not ship BEFORE per-trade EV is
        # positive in LIVE. Fix payoff (C397), re-measure on the next log, then
        # flip this to 50 as a single deliberate change with a clean A/B.
        # ═══ C429-1: 30 -> 60, ON THE OPERATOR'S DIRECTIVE ═══════════════════
        # C398 raised this to 50, reverted, and set a condition: widening ships
        # "only after the C397 payoff repairs are confirmed positive on a live
        # log". THAT CONDITION IS NOW MET and it was met by the C428 correction,
        # not by waiting. Across the fourteen exits of the three preceding
        # sessions: winners median +2.03%, losers median -0.64%, PAYOFF 3.2:1,
        # win rate 71%, expectancy +1.98R per trade against a break-even of
        # 24%. C389's failure mode -- widening a book that is NEGATIVE per
        # trade -- is the opposite of the present state.
        # AND THE CONSTRAINT IS REAL: the 20260830 log shows crypto with 448
        # contracts in its universe, 93-95 clearing the volume floor, and 29
